Erik reported this: the snippet treats WORKDIR/MERGED_BY as state that
survives across Bash tool calls. It does not, and this commit is the fix
— the framing is "they don't persist, here's how we carry them," not "we
wrongly assume."

I confirmed export does not rescue it: exported a var in one Bash tool
call, read it in the next, came back empty. Each call is a separate
shell process.

Alternatives weighed for WORKDIR before settling on mktemp:

A deterministic path under $(git rev-parse --git-dir) is recomputable
and collision-free, but in a worktree --git-dir is the per-worktree
private dir (.git/worktrees/<name>, vs --git-common-dir for the shared
one), so cleanup means rm-ing inside .git — a footgun agents avoid.
Rejected on that ground.

A fixed $TMPDIR/git-conflict-resolution path drops the .git problem but
collides globally across every repo and session on the machine.

A worktree-hashed $TMPDIR path is reproducible, which is its one real
advantage, but it still has to be remembered and injected per call
exactly like the mktemp literal — so it buys little over mktemp here.
Kept on the table as an alternative if reproducibility ever matters.

I verified the worktree --git-dir behavior in a throwaway repo, and
checked via bulk-catchup-rebase that the resolver runs as a sub-agent in
one shared working tree with conflict stops handled sequentially, so
parallel-worktree collisions are not a real scenario for this caller.

OP_TYPE and REPLAY_HEAD are fixed here too, at Erik's prompt. REPLAY_HEAD
is the one with teeth — it is consumed by git log in section 5. OP_TYPE
is only ever a prose label in the section-5 headers, never executed, but
it came from the same silent-assignment pattern, so it echoes now as
well.

